    {
      "id": "M8KMEjABNxr8mBTVPb-pV",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 4532.778302062354,
      "y": 616.2087269399598,
      "width": 668.7970726376489,
      "height": 1340,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0D",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 815252313,
      "version": 507,
      "versionNonce": 1096645335,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787361011270,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Absolutely. Here’s a polished interview-ready answer in English:\n\n“I’d describe the search flow in this project as a two-layer approach: a\nfast local search in the frontend and a server-side search endpoint\navailable in the API.\n\nOn the frontend, the input is bound to a  searchQuery  state in the Pinia\nstore. As the user types, the value updates reactively, and a computed\nproperty called  filteredRecipes  recalculates the visible list. The logic\nfilters the recipes by title and ingredients, and it also respects the\nselected category if one is active.\n\nIn practice, the flow is:\n\n1. user types in the search field\n2.  searchQuery  updates\n3. Vue re-renders the computed list\n4. only matching recipes remain visible\n\nThis makes the experience feel immediate and responsive, without making a\nbackend request for every character typed.\n\nThe actual frontend logic looks like this:\n\nconst searchQuery = ref('')\n\nconst filteredRecipes = computed(() => {\n  let result = recipes.value\n\n  if (activeCategory.value !== 'Todas') {\n    result = result.filter(r => r.category === activeCategory.value)\n  }\n\n  if (searchQuery.value.trim()) {\n    const q = searchQuery.value.toLowerCase()\n    result = result.filter(r =>\n      r.title.toLowerCase().includes(q) ||\n      r.ingredients.some(i => i.toLowerCase().includes(q))\n    )\n  }\n\n  return result\n})\n\nSo the first search happens in the frontend, based on the current recipe\ncollection in memory.\n\nAt the API level, we also support a server-side search by passing a  search\nquery parameter, for example:\n\nGET /api/v1/recipes?search=pasta\n\nOn the backend, the  RecipeList  action receives that parameter and applies\na SQL  LIKE  filter against the recipe title and description:\n\nif ($request->has('search')) {\n    $search = $request->search;\n    $query->where(function ($q) use ($search) {\n        $q->where('title', 'like', '%' . $search . '%')\n          ->orWhere('description', 'like', '%' . $search . '%');\n    });\n}\n\nSo the architecture is: the UI filters quickly and locally for\nresponsiveness, while the API still exposes a reusable search endpoint for\nserver-side filtering when needed. This keeps the user experience smooth\nand the backend logic clean, reusable, and easy to test.”",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Absolutely. Here’s a polished interview-ready answer in English:\n\n“I’d describe the search flow in this project as a two-layer approach: a fast local search in the frontend and a server-side search endpoint available in the API.\n\nOn the frontend, the input is bound to a  searchQuery  state in the Pinia store. As the user types, the value updates reactively, and a computed property called  filteredRecipes  recalculates the visible list. The logic filters the recipes by title and ingredients, and it also respects the selected category if one is active.\n\nIn practice, the flow is:\n\n1. user types in the search field\n2.  searchQuery  updates\n3. Vue re-renders the computed list\n4. only matching recipes remain visible\n\nThis makes the experience feel immediate and responsive, without making a backend request for every character typed.\n\nThe actual frontend logic looks like this:\n\nconst searchQuery = ref('')\n\nconst filteredRecipes = computed(() => {\n  let result = recipes.value\n\n  if (activeCategory.value !== 'Todas') {\n    result = result.filter(r => r.category === activeCategory.value)\n  }\n\n  if (searchQuery.value.trim()) {\n    const q = searchQuery.value.toLowerCase()\n    result = result.filter(r =>\n      r.title.toLowerCase().includes(q) ||\n      r.ingredients.some(i => i.toLowerCase().includes(q))\n    )\n  }\n\n  return result\n})\n\nSo the first search happens in the frontend, based on the current recipe collection in memory.\n\nAt the API level, we also support a server-side search by passing a  search  query parameter, for example:\n\nGET /api/v1/recipes?search=pasta\n\nOn the backend, the  RecipeList  action receives that parameter and applies a SQL  LIKE  filter against the recipe title and description:\n\nif ($request->has('search')) {\n    $search = $request->search;\n    $query->where(function ($q) use ($search) {\n        $q->where('title', 'like', '%' . $search . '%')\n          ->orWhere('description', 'like', '%' . $search . '%');\n    });\n}\n\nSo the architecture is: the UI filters quickly and locally for responsiveness, while the API still exposes a reusable search endpoint for server-side filtering when needed. This keeps the user experience smooth and the backend logic clean, reusable, and easy to test.”",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
