      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Backend\n\n• Ratings are stored in  recipe_ratings \n• each row is unique by  (recipe_id, user_id) \n• a user can rate a recipe only once, and the rating is updated if\nthey rate again\n\nThe backend endpoint is:\n\n•  POST /api/v1/recipes/{id}/ratings \n\nRequest:\n\n{\n  \"stars\": 5\n}\n\nThe logic is handled in a dedicated Action class, not directly in\nthe controller, and it updates the rating and then returns the\nupdated aggregate data like:\n\n• average rating\n• total count\n• user rating\n\nFrontend\n\n• the modal shows the current recipe and its ratings\n• the user clicks on stars\n•  StarRating  emits the value\n•  recipeStore.rateRecipe()  sends the request\n• then it replaces the local recipe object in the store with the\nupdated response\n\nThis is important because the UI must update immediately without a\nfull reload.\n\nExample flow:\n\n1. user clicks 4 stars\n2.  handleRate(stars)  calls  recipeStore.rateRecipe(recipeId,\nstars) \n3. backend saves to DB and returns new average/count\n4. frontend updates the same recipe in the local store\n5. grid and modal both respond to the new state\n\nOne key bug that was fixed here:\n\n• some components were using stale local data or wrong value\nsources\n• the read-only stars were using the user’s current model value\ninstead of the recipe average\n• the fix was to normalize values and use the recipe average in\nread-only mode\n\nImportant detail:\n\n• In the grid, the stars are shown based on the recipe average,\nnot on the current user’s own vote\n• in the modal, the user can click to rate, but the read-only\nstars still reflect the aggregate score\n\nSo in interview language I’d say:\n\nThe rating system is backed by a dedicated API endpoint and a\nbusiness Action on the backend. The frontend uses the  StarRating\ncomponent to submit the user’s vote, then updates the local recipe\nstate with the returned average and count, so the list and modal\nstay synchronized without a page refresh.",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Backend\n\n• Ratings are stored in  recipe_ratings \n• each row is unique by  (recipe_id, user_id) \n• a user can rate a recipe only once, and the rating is updated if they rate again\n\nThe backend endpoint is:\n\n•  POST /api/v1/recipes/{id}/ratings \n\nRequest:\n\n{\n  \"stars\": 5\n}\n\nThe logic is handled in a dedicated Action class, not directly in the controller, and it updates the rating and then returns the updated aggregate data like:\n\n• average rating\n• total count\n• user rating\n\nFrontend\n\n• the modal shows the current recipe and its ratings\n• the user clicks on stars\n•  StarRating  emits the value\n•  recipeStore.rateRecipe()  sends the request\n• then it replaces the local recipe object in the store with the updated response\n\nThis is important because the UI must update immediately without a full reload.\n\nExample flow:\n\n1. user clicks 4 stars\n2.  handleRate(stars)  calls  recipeStore.rateRecipe(recipeId, stars) \n3. backend saves to DB and returns new average/count\n4. frontend updates the same recipe in the local store\n5. grid and modal both respond to the new state\n\nOne key bug that was fixed here:\n\n• some components were using stale local data or wrong value sources\n• the read-only stars were using the user’s current model value instead of the recipe average\n• the fix was to normalize values and use the recipe average in read-only mode\n\nImportant detail:\n\n• In the grid, the stars are shown based on the recipe average, not on the current user’s own vote\n• in the modal, the user can click to rate, but the read-only stars still reflect the aggregate score\n\nSo in interview language I’d say:\n\nThe rating system is backed by a dedicated API endpoint and a business Action on the backend. The frontend uses the  StarRating  component to submit the user’s vote, then updates the local recipe state with the returned average and count, so the list and modal stay synchronized without a page refresh.",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "FdbKqFhQgoY-ZZ_d3U6Qt",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3691.2498906066285,
      "y": 889.6988719004135,
      "width": 582.1304059709823,
      "height": 320,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0E",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 9914105,
      "version": 808,
      "versionNonce": 263587831,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787361011270,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "In this app, the search starts on the frontend. The input is bound\nto a  searchQuery  in the Pinia store, and a computed\nfilteredRecipes  list recalculates as the user types. It filters\nrecipes by title and ingredients, and also respects the active\ncategory. This makes the UI very responsive without calling the\nAPI on every keystroke.\n\nThe backend also supports search through the API endpoint:\n\n•  GET /api/v1/recipes?search=bolo \n\nOn the server, the  RecipeList  action applies  LIKE  filters to\nthe recipe  title  and  description . So the architecture is: fast\nlocal filtering in the frontend for UX, and reusable server-side\nfiltering in the API for more controlled queries.\n",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "In this app, the search starts on the frontend. The input is bound to a  searchQuery  in the Pinia store, and a computed  filteredRecipes  list recalculates as the user types. It filters recipes by title and ingredients, and also respects the active category. This makes the UI very responsive without calling the API on every keystroke.\n\nThe backend also supports search through the API endpoint:\n\n•  GET /api/v1/recipes?search=bolo \n\nOn the server, the  RecipeList  action applies  LIKE  filters to the recipe  title  and  description . So the architecture is: fast local filtering in the frontend for UX, and reusable server-side filtering in the API for more controlled queries.\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "X-QYREihX6pG75MhqqPMY",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3692.7886779093515,
      "y": 1240.153371447826,
      "width": 597.6860293433775,
      "height": 380,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0F",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1214340697,
      "version": 874,
      "versionNonce": 1955059449,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787361011270,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Sure — here’s a short interview-ready answer in English:\n\n“The rating flow is handled by a dedicated API endpoint:  POST\n/api/v1/recipes/{id}/ratings . The backend stores the value in the\nrecipe_ratings  table, with a unique constraint per user and\nrecipe, so each user can only have one rating per recipe. The API\nthen returns the updated aggregate data, such as the new average\nrating and the total number of ratings.\n\nOn the frontend, the user clicks the stars in the modal, which\ntriggers the  StarRating  component. That value is sent to the\nstore, which calls the API and updates the recipe object in local\nstate with the new rating data. Because the store is shared, the\ngrid and modal stay synchronized without a page refresh.\n\nThe read-only stars in the list display the recipe average, while\nthe interactive stars in the modal are used to submit the user’s\nvote. This keeps the experience reactive and consistent across the\nUI.”",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Sure — here’s a short interview-ready answer in English:\n\n“The rating flow is handled by a dedicated API endpoint:  POST /api/v1/recipes/{id}/ratings . The backend stores the value in the  recipe_ratings  table, with a unique constraint per user and recipe, so each user can only have one rating per recipe. The API then returns the updated aggregate data, such as the new average rating and the total number of ratings.\n\nOn the frontend, the user clicks the stars in the modal, which triggers the  StarRating  component. That value is sent to the store, which calls the API and updates the recipe object in local state with the new rating data. Because the store is shared, the grid and modal stay synchronized without a page refresh.\n\nThe read-only stars in the list display the recipe average, while the interactive stars in the modal are used to submit the user’s vote. This keeps the experience reactive and consistent across the UI.”",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "EAjd60EwwS0bETkhyvbRs",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 3687.7034130473266,
      "y": 623.3517066746531,
      "width": 597.6860293433775,
      "height": 220,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "#eaddd7",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 4,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "b0G",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 492900729,
      "version": 1066,
      "versionNonce": 1505751319,
      "isDeleted": true,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787361011270,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Sure — short and interview-friendly:\n\n“The API is a RESTful Laravel backend with a single prefix:\n/api/v1 . It exposes public routes like login, register, and\nrecipes listing, and protected routes for authenticated actions\nsuch as creating recipes, updating them, deleting them, and rating\nthem. The auth flow uses JWT: the user logs in, the backend returns\nan access token, and the frontend stores it in localStorage. Then\nevery protected request sends the token in the  Authorization:\nBearer ...  header. The backend validates the token and authorizes\nthe request before processing it.”",
      "fontSize": 16,
      "fontFamily": 8,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Sure — short and interview-friendly:\n\n“The API is a RESTful Laravel backend with a single prefix:  /api/v1 . It exposes public routes like login, register, and recipes listing, and protected routes for authenticated actions such as creating recipes, updating them, deleting them, and rating them. The auth flow uses JWT: the user logs in, the backend returns an access token, and the frontend stores it in localStorage. Then every protected request sends the token in the  Authorization: Bearer ...  header. The backend validates the token and authorizes the request before processing it.”",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
